v	1
th	ROFFSUB	7	2026-11-15	roffsub r0.9	Miscellaneous Information
sh	NAME
tx	roffsub - a roff subset for manuals read in a terminal
sh	DESCRIPTION
tx	A roffsub document is a man page.
tx	Every roff program displays it: man, nroff, groff, mandoc.
tx	It is also simple enough for small awk and C routines to render from inside a program's own binary or bash function, at the width of the terminal in use, with bold and italic where the terminal can show them.
tx	One source then serves as the installed page and as the program's built-in help.
pp
tx	This page defines the source form, how it is rendered, and how a program presents it.
tx	The form is checked; content is not.
sh	ELEMENTS
ts	
tf	lb	lb	lb
tf	l	l	l
tr	ELEMENT	WRITTEN AS	SHOWN AS
t_
tr	title	.TH	header and footer, optional in help
tr	section	.SH	bold heading at the margin
tr	subsection	.SS	bold heading at half an indent
tr	paragraph	.PP .P .LP	filled text
tr	definition	.TP, tag on the next line	tag, body hanging at the indent
tr	list item	.IP	as a definition, tag inline
tr	nested block	.RS .RE	margin moved in and back
tr	literal block	.nf .fi	lines as written
tr	emphasis	font escapes and requests	bold literals, italic arguments
tr	spacing	.sp .br	blank lines, a line break
tr	table	.TS .TE	open columns, horizontal rules
tr	comment	.\\"	nothing
te
sh	SOURCE FORM
tx	Each rule states its purpose, so it can be judged by whether it achieves it.
ss	Lexical rules
ip	3	o
tx	Bytes 0x20 to 0x7E and newline only.
tx	A byte is then a column, so awk and C measure width with a plain length.
ip		o
tx	No tab characters.
tx	Layout is stated by requests, so every viewer shows the same thing, and tab is free to separate fields in the compiled form.
ip		o
tx	Line 1 is \fB'\\" t\fR when, and only when, the page has a table, so that man runs tbl first.
tx	It is the only line that begins with an apostrophe.
ip		o
tx	A control line is a period followed directly by a request listed below.
tx	A comment is a whole line beginning with a period, a backslash, and a double quote.
ip		o
tx	In fill mode a text line begins with a word: never a space, never empty, and never a period or apostrophe unless written after \\&.
tx	roff treats a leading space or a blank line as a layout instruction; stating breaks with requests gives them one meaning everywhere.
ip		o
tx	One sentence per line is recommended.
tx	Diffs stay small and sentence spacing stays predictable.
ss	Requests
tx	Widths are a bare number or a number followed by n, counting character cells.
pp
ts	
tf	lb	lb	lb
tf	l	l	l
tr	REQUEST	ARGUMENTS	MEANING
t_
tr	.TH	NAME SEC DATE SOURCE MANUAL	page title; first request; @DATE@ and @VERSION@ are filled in at install and compile
tr	.SH .SS	heading	section, subsection
tr	.PP .P .LP	none	new paragraph; resets the prevailing indent
tr	.TP	[width]	tagged paragraph; the next line, text or a font request, is the tag
tr	.IP	[tag [width]]	indented paragraph, optionally tagged
tr	.RS	[width]	move the margin in by width or by the prevailing indent
tr	.RE	none	restore the margin
tr	.nf .fi	none	literal and fill mode; literal blocks hold text only
tr	.B .I	words	bold or italic words
tr	.BR .RB .IR .RI .BI .IB	words	two fonts alternating word by word
tr	.sp	[N]	N blank lines, default 1
tr	.br	none	line break
tr	.TS .TE	none	table
te
pp
tx	Double quotes group words into one argument; a quote inside is written \\(dq.
tx	A request takes at most 9 arguments.
ss	Escapes
ts	
tf	lb	lb	lb
tf	l	l	l
tr	ESCAPE	MEANING	TERMINAL OUTPUT
t_
tr	\\fB \\fI \\fR	bold, italic, roman	font change
tr	\\fP	previous font	font change
tr	\\-	minus, copies as a real hyphen	-
tr	\\e	backslash	\\
tr	\\&	zero-width guard	nothing
tr	\\(space)	space that never breaks	space
tr	\\(em	em dash	--
tr	\\(en	en dash	-
tr	\\(bu	bullet	o
tr	\\(aq	apostrophe	'
tr	\\(dq	double quote	"
te
pp
tx	The font returns to roman by the end of every request line and table cell, so emphasis never runs past where it was meant to stop.
ss	Tables
nf
tx	.TS
tx	box tab(@);
tx	lb lb
tx	l  l.
tx	OPTION@MEANING
tx	_
tx	\\fB\\-r\\fR@reverse the order
tx	.TE
fi
pp
tx	The options line is required and ends with a semicolon.
tx	It names the cell separator with tab(c), a printable character that is not a letter or digit and appears in no cell, and may add box, allbox, and center.
tx	Format lines follow, the last ending with a period; keys are l, r, and c, with modifiers b, i, and w(N), a minimum width.
tx	Format line i governs data row i and the last repeats.
tx	Each data row has one cell per column; a row of a single underscore is a rule.
tx	A cell opened with T{ continues on the following lines until a line beginning T}, and is filled within its column.
tx	roff wraps only these text blocks, so any cell that may need wrapping is written as one; the roffsub renderers wrap every cell.
tx	The page installed for man is written by roffsub -n, which rewrites each table so that roff draws it the same way: an empty separator column between the columns, rules joined by a + in those columns, no vertical lines, and columns holding text blocks widened to fill the line.
ss	Content
tx	A conventional order is NAME as \fIname\fR \\- \fIsummary\fR, then SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, OPTIONS, EXAMPLES, ENVIRONMENT, FILES, EXIT STATUS, SEE ALSO.
tx	The order is guidance; content belongs to the author.
sh	RENDERING
ss	Width and margins
tx	Text fills to the width with a right margin of 0, or to the measure when one is set.
tx	Words are packed greedily with a ragged right edge; a word too long for its line runs past the margin whole.
tx	Literal lines are never rewrapped.
tx	The indent unit is 7 cells, or 4 when the width is under 60.
ss	Indentation and spacing
ip	3	o
tx	A section heading sits at the margin and its body one unit in; a subsection heading sits half a unit in.
ip		o
tx	Each paragraph, tagged paragraph, and indented paragraph is preceded by one blank line, except directly after a heading.
ip		o
tx	A tag shorter than the prevailing indent shares its line with the body; a tag as long or longer puts the body on the next line.
ip		o
tx	A width given to .TP or .IP becomes the prevailing indent for later items until the next .SH, .SS, or .PP.
ip		o
tx	.RS saves the margin and prevailing indent, moves the margin in, and resets the prevailing indent; .RE restores both.
ss	Tables
tx	Tables draw no vertical lines.
tx	Each cell has one space of padding on each side and columns meet at one separator position, a space in data rows and a plus sign in rules, so column text sits three cells apart.
tx	Rules appear where the source has an underscore row; box adds one above and below, allbox one after every row.
pp
tx	When a table is wider than the space available, the widest columns give up width first, down to a common cap, never below their longest word, and short columns keep their natural width.
tx	Cells wrap within their columns.
tx	When even the longest words do not fit, each row is shown as lines of the form \fIheader\fR: \fIvalue\fR.
ss	Emphasis
ts	
tf	lb	lb	lb	lb
tf	l	l	l	l
tr	STYLE	USED FOR	BOLD	ITALIC
t_
tr	sgr	a terminal	ESC[1m	ESC[4m, underline
tr	overstrike	a pager	c BS c	_ BS c
tr	plain	a file, a pipe, TERM=dumb	none	none
tr	pre	pre-formatting	\\fB	\\fI
te
pp
tx	Control bytes are added only as a line is written, so they never count toward its width.
tx	Emphasis is closed before every newline and reopened on the next line.
tx	The SGR parameters may be replaced, so that bold and italic show as colors.
sh	PROGRAM INTERFACE
ss	Help
tx	\fB--help\fR presents the full manual.
tx	What it writes is fixed by four inputs: the embedded manual, the width, the style, and the destination.
tx	The C and bash forms of a program write the same bytes for the same inputs.
pp
ts	
tf	lb	lb	lb	lb
tf	l	l	l	l
tr	OUTPUT	GOES TO	STYLE	WIDTH
t_
tr	terminal	the pager	overstrike	terminal
tr	terminal, PAGER is cat	the terminal	sgr	terminal
tr	file or pipe	the file or pipe	plain	80
te
pp
tx	The pager is PAGER when set, otherwise less, otherwise more.
tx	The width is MANWIDTH when set, otherwise COLUMNS, otherwise the terminal's own.
tx	ROFFSUB_STYLE, ROFFSUB_SGR, and ROFFSUB_MEASURE override the style, the emphasis parameters, and the measure; TERM=dumb selects plain.
tx	ROFFSUB_HEADING moves section headings that many columns in from the margin, leaving everything else in place.
ss	Usage
tx	\fB-h\fR serves a different purpose from the manual: a quick reference, a refresher and confirmation when a full review is not needed.
tx	It is composed for that purpose, not extracted from the manual.
ss	Profiles
tx	A man page is built from the source and installed in every case.
tx	Inside the program the manual is carried in one of three profiles.
pp
ts	
tf	lb	lb	lb
tf	l	l	l
tr	PROFILE	THE PROGRAM CARRIES	HELP GIVES
t_
tr	full	records and the renderer	any width, emphasis by terminal
tr	preformatted	text set at 80 columns, fonts kept, and the emitter	fixed width, emphasis by terminal
tr	plain	text set at 80 columns	fixed width
te
pp
tx	The full profile is used when the renderer is no larger than the program it would live in, not counting the manual, and an exported bash function stays within its size budget; otherwise preformatted.
tx	Plain is used only when a program asks for it.
sh	COMPILED FORM
tx	One record per line: an operation, then fields separated by tabs.
tx	The operations are th, sh, ss, pp, tp, ip, rs, re, nf, fi, tx, sp, br, and for tables ts, tf, tr, t_, and te; the first record is v 1.
tx	Text fields carry only \\fB, \\fI, \\fR, \\\\, and \\~, and end in roman.
tx	Records are passed to awk on its standard input, never with -v, which would rewrite their backslashes.
sh	CONFORMANCE
ts	
tf	lb	lb
tf	l	l
tr	ID	RULE
t_
tr	E01	byte outside 0x20 to 0x7E
tr	E02	request not in roffsub, or an apostrophe control line
tr	E03	escape not in roffsub
tr	E04	unbalanced .nf/.fi, .RS/.RE, .TS/.TE, or T{/T}
tr	E05	fill-mode text line empty or beginning with a space
tr	E06	font not roman at a request line or cell end
tr	E07	the table line and the tables disagree
tr	E08	table form
tr	E09	.TH missing or not the first request
tr	E10	request arguments
tr	E11	.TP without a tag line
tr	W01	option hyphen written without \\-
tr	W02	abbreviation ending a line without \\&
tr	W03	table wider than 71 columns with no text blocks to wrap
te
sh	EXAMPLES
tx	Each example shows its source, then the same source rendered in place.
ss	Open table with a header rule
nf
tx	.TS
tx	tab(@);
tx	lb lb
tx	l  l.
tx	OPTION@MEANING
tx	_
tx	\\fB\\-r\\fR@reverse the order
tx	\\fB\\-k\\fR \\fIkey\\fR@sort on \\fIkey\\fR
tx	.TE
fi
pp
ts	
tf	lb	lb
tf	l	l
tr	OPTION	MEANING
t_
tr	\fB-r\fR	reverse the order
tr	\fB-k\fR \fIkey\fR	sort on \fIkey\fR
te
ss	Boxed table, aligned columns
tx	The box option adds rules above and below; keys r and c align right and center.
pp
nf
tx	.TS
tx	box tab(@);
tx	lb cb rb
tx	l  c  r.
tx	NAME@KIND@SIZE
tx	_
tx	alpha@file@12
tx	beta@directory@3456
tx	.TE
fi
pp
ts	box
tf	lb	cb	rb
tf	l	c	r
tr	NAME	KIND	SIZE
t_
tr	alpha	file	12
tr	beta	directory	3456
te
ss	Ruled table, centered
tx	The allbox option puts a rule after every row; center places the table in the middle of the line.
pp
nf
tx	.TS
tx	allbox center tab(@);
tx	cb cb
tx	r  l.
tx	CODE@MEANING
tx	0@success
tx	1@a check found errors
tx	2@a usage or input error
tx	.TE
fi
pp
ts	allbox center
tf	cb	cb
tf	r	l
tr	CODE	MEANING
tr	0	success
tr	1	a check found errors
tr	2	a usage or input error
te
ss	Text blocks that wrap
tx	A cell written between T{ and T} wraps within its column, in roff and in the roffsub renderers alike.
pp
nf
tx	.TS
tx	tab(@);
tx	lb lb
tx	l  l.
tx	TERM@DEFINITION
tx	_
tx	measure@T{
tx	the longest line that filled text may reach,
tx	whatever the width of the terminal
tx	T}
tx	prevailing indent@T{
tx	the indent that tagged and indented paragraphs use
tx	when they give none of their own
tx	T}
tx	.TE
fi
pp
ts	
tf	lb	lb
tf	l	l
tr	TERM	DEFINITION
t_
tr	measure	the longest line that filled text may reach, whatever the width of the terminal
tr	prevailing indent	the indent that tagged and indented paragraphs use when they give none of their own
te
ss	Minimum column width
tx	A key with w(N) keeps its column at least N cells wide.
pp
nf
tx	.TS
tx	tab(@);
tx	lw(12) l.
tx	key@value
tx	.TE
fi
pp
ts	
tf	lw(12)	l
tr	key	value
te
ss	Definitions with a narrow indent
tx	A width on .TP sets the prevailing indent for the items that follow.
pp
nf
tx	.TP 4
tx	.B a
tx	first
tx	.TP
tx	.B b
tx	second
fi
tp	4	\fBa\fR
tx	first
tp		\fBb\fR
tx	second
ss	Numbered list and nested block
nf
tx	.IP 1. 4
tx	Write the page.
tx	.IP 2.
tx	Check it:
tx	.RS
tx	.nf
tx	roffsub \\-c page.1
tx	.fi
tx	.RE
fi
ip	4	1.
tx	Write the page.
ip		2.
tx	Check it:
rs	
nf
tx	roffsub -c page.1
fi
re
ss	Emphasis
nf
tx	.BR roffsub (1)
tx	reads \\fIfile\\fR with \\fB\\-w\\fR \\fIwidth\\fR.
fi
pp
tx	\fBroffsub\fR(1)
tx	reads \fIfile\fR with \fB-w\fR \fIwidth\fR.
sh	SEE ALSO
tx	\fBroffsub\fR(1),
tx	\fBman\fR(7),
tx	\fBtbl\fR(1)
